 short phrases within the same paragraph. Three pairs are concentrated in the \"A Calibration of Scale and Craft\" section, where the budget sentence, the IMAX-anticipation sentence, and the IMAX-shot-entirely sentence cycle through the same production and format vocabulary without advancing distinct claims. A fourth pair is in \"Systemic Integration in Exhibition,\" where the summer trailing sentence and the dominant-position sentence overlap.\n\n**WARNING ‚Äî demand-lens-missing-h2.**\nNo level-two heading naturally expresses the central event's significance (Nolan's biggest global debut). \"A Calibration of Scale and Craft\" and \"Systemic Integration in Exhibition\" are abstract; neither signals what a reader seeking the debut story would expect to find. This is a warning, not a hard gate failure, but it compounds the near-duplicate weakness.\n\n---\n\n### Material Factual Audit\n\n**Lede and debut figure: supported.**\nThe $264.1 million global debut is confirmed across ToI ($124.5M domestic + $139.6M international), Variety (same figures), LA Times (studio estimates), and Deadline ($264.1M worldwide, ahead of Dark Knight Rises at $249M WW). The framing as Nolan's biggest global opening is warranted by all four sources.\n\n**Budget ($200M‚Äì$250M): supported.**\nLA Times and ToI both report this range; the manuscript cites both sources for this claim. ‚úì\n\n**Shot entirely with IMAX cameras: supported.**\nLA Times and ToI both confirm this as the first feature shot entirely on Imax cameras. ‚úì\n\n**Practical effects (animatronics, Cyclops, Viking boat): supported.**\nLA Times source describes puppetry, animatronics, robotics for Cyclops scenes and a real Viking boat the actors sailed. The manuscript describes this as \"a deliberate departure from contemporary digital-heavy standards,\" which imports an editorial gloss not present in the source text; the source characterizes it as adding \"mystique,\" not as a departure statement. Borderline but not a clear misrepresentation.\n\n**95% critical approval: supported (attribution slightly loose).**\nToI says \"95% fresh on Rotten Tomatoes.\" The manuscript says \"95% critical approval rating\" without naming Rotten Tomatoes. The omission weakens attribution but is not a fabrication.\n\n**IMAX 4% claim: FACTUAL MISREPRESENTATION.**\nThe manuscript states: \"IMAX, in particular, played a central role in the film's financial performance, with the format accounting for 4% of the total box office haul.\"\n\nThe LA Times source (evidence card `bd7c6f1462b3ca8f4d623b38b4e627013f9405d16a050668ba9fa10022d4c550:16`) specifies: \"Enhanced formats made up 53% of the domestic weekend's total, with both film and digital Imax revenue comprising 23.8%. Imax 70mm comprised about 4% of that total, with Imax digital making up the rest.\"\n\n\"That total\" refers to the enhanced-format portion (53% of domestic gross), not the overall box office. Imax 70mm was approximately 4% of 53% ‚âà 2% of domestic gross ‚Äî a sub-format share within a sub-total. The manuscript treats this sub-category share as if it were all of IMAX's contribution to the overall haul. By contrast, the ToI source reports IMAX grossed $51.8 million globally ‚Äî approximately 19.6% of the $264.1 million total ‚Äî which sharply contradicts a \"4% of total\" reading. The manuscript's sentence materially understates and mislabels IMAX's contribution.\n\n**Summer box office comparison: FACTUAL MISREPRESENTATION.**\nThe manuscript states: \"summer-to-date box office revenue is trailing 2019 levels by roughly 7% to 9%.\"\n\nThe LA Times source (evidence card `bd7c6f1462b3ca8f4d623b38b4e627013f9405d16a050668ba9fa10022d4c550:19`) reports two distinct numbers: summer-to-date is \"about 7% higher than the same time period last year\" AND \"still lags pre-pandemic summer 2019 revenue by about 9%.\" These are different comparators ‚Äî one is a year-over-year gain, the other is the deficit against a pre-pandemic baseline. The manuscript collapses them into a single \"7% to 9%\" range that describes only trailing 2019, falsely converting the year-over-year improvement into a second measure of the 2019 deficit.\n\n---\n\n### Citation Link Text Audit\n\nWriting law requires exact character-for-character reproduction of source titles in link text. Every inline citation in this manuscript violates that requirement.\n\n**Times of India title (appears 5 times in body):**\n- Source title: `'The Odyssey' storms box office with a USD 264.1 million global debut; dethrones 'The Dark Knight Rises' as Christopher Nolan's highest film opening`\n- Manuscript link text: `The Odyssey storms box office with a USD 264.1 million global debut, dethrones The Dark Knight Rises as Christopher Nolan's highest film opening`\n- Alterations: apostrophes around both film names removed; semicolon after \"global debut\" replaced with comma.\n\n**Deadline title (appears once in body):**\n- Source title: `'The Odyssey' Conquers $264M+ Around The World, Best Ever For Christopher Nolan Ahead Of 'Dark Knight Rises' ‚Äì Global B.O. Sunday Update`\n- Manuscript link text: `The Odyssey conquers $264M around the world, best ever for Christopher Nolan ahead of Dark Knight Rises ‚Äì Global B.O. Sunday Update`\n- Alterations: apostrophes removed; \"Conquers\" ‚Üí \"conquers\"; \"$264M+\" ‚Üí \"$264M\" (drops the \"+\" indicating the figure exceeded that threshold); title-case \"The World,\" \"Best,\" \"Ever,\" \"For,\" \"Ahead,\" \"Of\" ‚Üí sentence case throughout.\n\n**LA Times title (appears 6 times in body):**\n- Source title: `'The Odyssey' is Christopher Nolan's biggest global box office opening`\n- Manuscript link text: `Christopher Nolan's 'The Odyssey' box office`\n- Alteration: entire title replaced with a shortened informal paraphrase.\n\nThese are not typographic slips ‚Äî every citation is systematically altered. The prevalence across all three sources and all 12 body citation instances points to a systemic writer-model behavior, not an isolated error.\n\n---\n\n### Carrier Surface Defects\n\nThe `description` field is truncated mid-sentence: \"The latest feature film from Christopher Nolan, The Odyssey, has set a new benchmark for the director's career by securing a $264.\" The sentence ends at the dollar amount without the number.\n\nThe `summary` field contains: \"...securing a $264. O.\" ‚Äî a corrupted token sequence ‚Äî followed by a sentence not found in the body, ending with what appears to be a floating source title fragment (\"Christopher Nolan's 'The Odyssey' box office\").\n\nThe `tweet` field reproduces the headline verbatim rather than functioning as a share sentence.\n\nThese surface defects are consistent with writer-model output truncation and would be regenerated by `repair_editorial_surfaces` ‚Äî but only after the body's factual errors are corrected, since surfaces are projected from admitted body content.\n\n---\n\n### Fairness Assessment\n\nThe manuscript briefly reports the casting controversy (Elon Musk's comments on Lupita Nyong'o's casting) and characterizes it as having \"little to no effect\" on the film's performance ‚Äî a framing directly attributed to the ToI source. No allegation of wrongdoing is lodged against any individual; the controversy note is a contextual observation with source backing. No fairness obligation is triggered.\n\n---\n\n### Replay Scope\n\nA writer replay is required. The citation link text corruption is distributed across all three sources and 12 body locations; exact bounded repair across 10+ unique spans is operationally infeasible without unacceptable risk of partial repair collisions, especially given the simultaneous need to expand word count and rewrite near-duplicate pairs. The two factual corrections are individually bounded, but performing them before a writer replay that would rewrite the same sections wastes editorial work. The writer replay should produce: a body exceeding 600 words with no duplicate phrase repetition across adjacent sentences; correct citation link texts reproducing source titles character-for-character; and factually accurate representations of the IMAX format revenue breakdown and the year-over-year versus 2019 summer box office comparisons.\n"}ÉdpairÉhconstantÇdbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;RO9paInYepjRrlVutw7rSGkmeugA5G_m4Y5I09ZcQmgÉdpairÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉdleftÅdstarÅdunitÇdmarkx9ni:///sha-256;Hp5_QSZ3m8WRyi1XlTPO09fFSX6KxwMnQIO6jG3hVhMÉdpairÉhconstantÇdbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;r0EKumZMrWrCsYCRFxfTmC2Vt1h3hsHXh-aGXPtiDIwÉdpairÉdleftÅdstarÉcsumÅdunitÉcsumÅdunitÉcsumÅdunitÉcsumÅdunitÉcsumÅdunitÉcsumÅdunitÅdunitÇdmark`ÉdpairÉhconstantÇdbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;YZv3R_JUU0MonBn0nXNiDnwhRSX5vBOiO3XWC25Q8vAÉdpairÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉdleftÅdstarÅdunitÇdmarkxclaude-code-cli-invocationsÉdpairÉhconstantÇdbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Pkpv3TfXT6xN_30XwX_4HJHAEI0krFvc1FQrZ7GgqkoÉdpairÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉdleftÅdstarÉcsumÅdunitÉcsumÅdunitÅdunitÇdmarka1ÉdpairÉhconstantÇdbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;URerjBAhFKVWxht0bSEIwqu7eEp9fzm18VKs_k203_4ÉdpairÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉdleftÅdstarÉcsumÅdunitÅdunitÇdmarkg766.993ÉdpairÉhconstantÇdbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;xv0PPHEy2yhSbovAONmt0jKTDl_pnbWiYmOQ1QAcEZAÉdpairÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉdleftÅdstarÉcsumÅdunitÉcsumÅdunitÉcsumÅdunitÅdunitÇdmarkefalseÉdpairÉhconstantÇdbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;0AyCyorAhg-8aKesR2TB-7sLDGjZAQtGWHf2m5k9qUIÉdpairÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉdleftÅdstarÅdunitÇdmarkx9ni:///sha-256;xwl81mykurx0mGdCPzrPlhHCnOgHseBJylb5FNMDC0gÉdpairÉhconstantÇdbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;SXCX32VRC0RukE_eRfbt8uHqo61SXFLKsfpp7oT12KgÉdpairÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉdleftÅdstarÅdunitÇdmarkxexchange-20260720-054753-abab29ÉdpairÉhconstan